The package ggml provides CMake integration:

  find_package(ggml CONFIG REQUIRED)
  target_link_libraries(main PRIVATE ggml::ggml)

Windows builds enable LTO. Libraries built with clang-cl require a compatible
LLVM toolchain and lld-link in the consuming application.

Available vcpkg features:
  metal  - Metal GPU backend (macOS/iOS, auto-enabled on Apple)
  vulkan - Vulkan GPU backend
  cuda   - CUDA GPU backend
  opencl - OpenCL GPU backend

Server CUDA builds target:
  75-virtual;80-virtual;80-real;86-real;89-real;90-virtual;120a-real;121a-real

The virtual targets contain PTX for driver JIT compilation; real targets
contain native GPU code. T4 (7.5) is covered by PTX, so it can use CUDA when
the installed driver/runtime can load the code. This feature does not build
a separate Jetson CUDA module.

Release builds use CUDA 13.0.3 (nvcc 13.0.88). With QVAC_CUDA_TOOLKIT set,
the marker must be 13.0.3-server and nvcc must match. Other local toolkits
>= 12.9 warn and are not release builds. CUDACXX selects nvcc explicitly;
otherwise CUDA_PATH selects the toolkit before /usr/local/cuda and PATH.
Consumer triplets must hash QVAC_CUDA_TOOLKIT with VCPKG_ENV_PASSTHROUGH
and preserve CUDACXX/CUDA_PATH with VCPKG_ENV_PASSTHROUGH_UNTRACKED.

On Linux the CUDA backend is a runtime-loaded module. Enable vulkan as well
to retain GPU fallback when CUDA cannot load or has no compatible device.
Automatic selection can then use Vulkan, or CPU if no GPU is available.
